>
  <h2 id="what">What a good bug video shows</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A known starting point.</strong> Start from a page or screen anyone can open: the URL, a fresh launch, or right after signing in.</li>
    <li><strong>Every step, at normal speed.</strong> Don't skip ahead. The step you think doesn't matter is often the one that does.</li>
    <li><strong>The address bar and any error.</strong> For a web app, keep the browser's address bar in the shot. If the developer tools console shows an error, open it before you start.</li>
    <li><strong>What you expected.</strong> Say it out loud, or write it in the ticket: "I expected the invoice to save; it showed a blank page."</li>
    <li><strong>Short.</strong> Under a minute is ideal. If it takes three tries to make the bug appear, keep only the try that did.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="steps">Record it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Open the picker</strong> with <kbd>⌥</kbd><kbd>⇧</kbd><kbd>R</kbd> and choose the app's window. Choose the whole display if the bug involves more than one window.</li>
    <li><strong>Turn on Microphone</strong> if you'll narrate, and <strong>System audio</strong> if the bug makes a sound.</li>
    <li><strong>Turn on Record keystrokes</strong> in <strong>Settings… → Recording</strong> if typing or shortcuts matter. The overlay shows shortcuts; typed text only if you turn it on.</li>
    <li><strong>Start recording</strong> and walk through the steps.</li>
    <li><strong>Press <kbd>⌥</kbd><kbd>⇧</kbd><kbd>M</kbd> the moment it breaks.</strong> That drops a marker, so the developer can jump straight there.</li>
    <li><strong>Stop</strong> with <kbd>⌥</kbd><kbd>⇧</kbd><kbd>R</kbd>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="steps-text">Get the steps written out for you</h2>
  <p>Reelbird logs each click, run of typing and shortcut while you record. Open the take's <strong>Guide</strong> panel (<kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>9</kbd>) and they're already a numbered list of steps, each with a picture of the screen just before it. Rewrite a step or hide the ones that don't matter. On a share link they show as a Guide and a Click-through next to the video, and you can export them as Markdown to paste under "Steps to reproduce". More in <a href="/features/step-by-step-guides/">step-by-step guides</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="private">Blur what shouldn't be in a ticket</h2>
  <p>Bug reports often show real customer data. <a href="/features/blur-sensitive-info/">Find Sensitive Info</a> reads the screen on your Mac and suggests blurs for emails, API keys and tokens, card numbers and phone numbers, following the text as it scrolls. Press <kbd>B</kbd> to blur anything else by hand. Blurs are drawn into the exported video and the shared one, and can't be reversed from them.</p>

  <h2 id="share">Put it in the ticket</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A link</strong> is easiest: <kbd>⇧</kbd><kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>E</kbd> shares the take and copies the link. Paste it into Jira, Linear, GitHub or an email. Anyone with the link can watch it, with no account.</li>
    <li><strong>A file</strong> if your tracker wants an attachment: <kbd>⌘</kbd><kbd>E</kbd> exports MP4, and HEVC keeps it smaller. <a href="/guides/reduce-screen-recording-file-size-mac/">Making a recording smaller</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>A GIF</strong> for a GitHub issue or a README, where a short loop plays inline. <a href="/guides/screen-recording-to-gif-mac/">Screen recording to GIF</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you edit the take after sharing it, say to trim it or blur something you missed, choose <strong>Update link</strong>: the link already in the ticket plays the new version.</p>

  <h2 id="agents">Hand it to a coding agent</h2>
  <p>If the person fixing it uses Claude Code, Codex or Cursor, Reelbird's MCP server lets the agent read the take as text: the steps, what you said and your markers, without watching the video. See <a href="/use-cases/dev-demos/">coding and PR demos</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">Related questions</h2>
  <h3>How long should a bug report video be?</h3>
  <p>As short as it can be while still starting from a known point. Under a minute is usually enough. Trim the start and end, and cut failed tries with <kbd>S</kbd> and <kbd>Delete</kbd>.</p>
  <h3>Should I narrate a bug report?</h3>
  <p>It helps: say what you expected and what happened. If you'd rather not, write it in the ticket. The steps list covers what you did either way.</p>
  <h3>Can I record a bug report on iPhone with this?</h3>
  <p>No. Reelbird records the Mac's own screen, on macOS 15 or later.</p>
</Article>
